  "response": "The district adopted a structured phonics literacy curriculum in 2019, and in the four years after that adoption third-grade reading proficiency in the district rose 14 percentage points. That rise shows that a statistically significant pre-post score gain by itself establishes that the curriculum caused the improvement. By contrast, the district hired additional reading interventionists in the same year the curriculum was introduced.\n\nAdjudication among rival accounts in this setting is therefore a matter of research design rather than of larger samples. Researchers compared the district's test score trajectory with that of demographically similar districts that did not adopt the curriculum, and routing the inference through such a comparison is precisely what produced a new identifying requirement: difference-in-differences designs rely on the assumption that treated and comparison units would have followed parallel trends absent the intervention. Event-study plots report year-by-year estimates relative to the year of adoption. Whereas the credibility of such a design is ordinarily said to hinge on the premise of parallel trajectories, difference-in-differences estimates remain unbiased when pre-adoption trends diverge between districts.\n\nThe interpretive problem can be stated sharply. At least one of two things changed after 2019 \u2014 classroom reading instruction in the district, or the composition of students tested in the district \u2014 and perhaps both changed together. Because the district's enrollment of English learners declined across the study period, researchers were prompted to examine whether the demographic composition of tested students shifted after the adoption year. The decline in English learner enrollment indicates that the pool of students sitting for the assessment did in fact change after the adoption year, so the second possibility cannot be set aside on the strength of the headline proficiency figures.\n\nOnce the comparison districts, the year-by-year estimates and the composition checks are brought together, the magnitude of the result can be stated with some precision. The estimated effect of the curriculum was 0.21 standard deviations in student-level reading scores; that is, the curriculum's measured impact amounted to roughly one-fifth of a standard deviation in reading achievement. Researchers then ask whether an effect of that size survives alternative specifications, alternative comparison groups and alternative ways of handling the tested population, since a figure that moves substantially under such perturbations is telling us about the design rather than about the classroom. Adjudication, in short, consists in making each rival account commit to an observable implication and then asking which implications the data actually bear out.",
